Theorem IC-4: ReturnContract per-dim join over return paths

  Preconditions:
    - IC-1 has decomposed the CallGraph into SCCs in forward
      topological order (callees before callers) per
      Annex E §AIMS IC-1 + sec-7 PL-1a
    - IC-2 (proven separately) seeds ParamContract bottoms before
      any join fires; IC-3 (proven separately) provides the
      componentwise join used to PROMOTE param contracts toward
      conservative across call sites
    - IC-4 type signature (per Interprocedural.proof lines 31-34):
        join_return_contract : List ReturnContract -> ReturnContract
      whose output is the componentwise join over all
      Return-terminator paths within the function, iterated to
      the SCC fixpoint
    - Spec statement (Annex E §AIMS IC-4): ReturnContract joins uniqueness,
      locality, and shape by their lattice joins; preserves_freshness and
      returns_fresh_self_alloc are path-universal AND facts.
    - Per-path classification table (Annex E §AIMS.9 Return
      Provenance) enumerates the structural definition shape ->
      return tuple `(uniqueness, preserves_freshness,
      returns_fresh_self_alloc, locality, shape)`
      for every ArcInstr kind reachable from a `Return { value }`
      terminator
    - Shipped engine: `extract_return_info` at
      compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/interprocedural/
      extract.rs lines 790-839; recursive helper `var_uniqueness`
      lines 844-913 performs the structural trace
    - Per-dimension `join` operators are the lattice joins:
      - `uniqueness(join)` = `std::cmp::max` over chain
        `Unique < MaybeShared < Shared` per Annex E §AIMS.4
      - `locality(join)` = `std::cmp::max` over chain
        `BlockLocal < FunctionLocal < ArgEscaping < HeapEscaping <
        Unknown` per Annex E §AIMS.5
      - `shape(join)` = ShapeClass join per Annex E §AIMS.6
    - `preserves_freshness` is a `bool` joined by `AND` per
      Annex E §AIMS IC-4 "Freshness requires ALL paths
      preserve" — the conjunction-monoid identity element is
      `true`, hence ALL-paths preservation
    - `returns_fresh_self_alloc` is a distinct `bool` joined by AND. It is
      false for parameter passthrough and possible consumed-storage takeover,
      even when preserves_freshness remains true. RL-29 consumes only this
      stronger provenance fact
    - Target-vs-shipped axis carve-out (per Annex E §AIMS
      "Target-only / partially-shipped subsystems" top-of-file +
      §AIMS §7 Return Provenance): shipped `extract_return_info`
      computes `uniqueness`, `preserves_freshness`, and
      `returns_fresh_self_alloc` exactly; the
      `locality` + `shape` dimensions default to CONSERVATIVE
      (`ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE`) until the full locality/shape
      extractor lands. The shipped semantic subset rides on the same
      componentwise-monotone discipline
    - Parameter passthrough may preserve freshness while remaining caller-
      rooted. The separate returns_fresh_self_alloc=false field records that
      distinction and prevents RL-29 from treating it as a self-allocation
    - L-1, L-2, L-4, L-6, L-8 lemmas from §AIMS §3 are proven in
      sec-02 of this plan (Plan-Bug DAG) and provide
      commutativity, associativity, partial-order axiom,
      monotonicity, and canonicalization-preserves-join for the
      per-dim lattice joins

  Soundness property:
    Forall function `f` with N >= 0 `Return { value }` terminators
    and corresponding per-path ReturnContract list
    `[rc_1, rc_2, ..., rc_N]`.
      Let `r := join_return_contract([rc_1, ..., rc_N])` be the
      emitted result. Seven conjuncts SHALL hold per spec
      Annex E §AIMS IC-4 + Annex E §AIMS.9 Return
      Provenance:

      (P1) Uniqueness componentwise max join:
        r.uniqueness = max(rc_1.uniqueness, ...,
                           rc_N.uniqueness)
        under the chain order `Unique < MaybeShared < Shared`
        per §AIMS §3.4. For N = 0 (no Return terminators —
        e.g., infinite loop), `r = ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE`
        per the empty-fold identity at extract.rs line 837.

      (P2) preserves_freshness AND over all paths:
        r.preserves_freshness = rc_1.preserves_freshness AND
                                rc_2.preserves_freshness AND
                                ... AND rc_N.preserves_freshness
        per sec-5 IC-4 "Freshness requires ALL paths preserve".
        For N = 0, `r.preserves_freshness = true` by the AND-monoid
        identity element, then forced to
        `ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE.preserves_freshness`
        (= false) by the N=0 fast path (extract.rs line 837);
        this preserves "ALL paths" vacuously while emitting the
        CONSERVATIVE seed downstream consumers require.

      (P2a) returns_fresh_self_alloc AND over all paths:
        r.returns_fresh_self_alloc is true iff every return path yields
        fresh storage with no upstream alias, directly or through a callee
        carrying the same path-universal proof. A
        passthrough, borrowed view, or possible consumed-storage takeover on
        any path sticky-clears the fact. RL-29 consumes this field exclusively.

      (P3) Locality componentwise max join:
        r.locality = max(rc_1.locality, ..., rc_N.locality)
        under the chain order `BlockLocal < FunctionLocal <
        ArgEscaping < HeapEscaping < Unknown` per §AIMS §3.5.
        Shipped extractor defaults this dim to
        `CONSERVATIVE.locality` (target-vs-shipped axis
        carve-out); spec join shape is preserved when the locality extractor
        lands.

      (P4) Shape componentwise join:
        r.shape = ShapeClass-join over the rc_i.shape values
        per Annex E §AIMS.6 ShapeClass lattice. Same
        target-vs-shipped axis carve-out as (P3).

      Plus two lattice-property conjuncts inherited from
      §AIMS §3 + standard product-lattice algebra:

      (P5) Empty-fold identity:
        N = 0 -> r = ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE
        per the explicit None-arm at extract.rs line 837.
        Conservative is the lattice TOP for ReturnContract;
        N = 0 means "no path actually returns", so downstream
        consumers MUST treat the return as conservative —
        cannot prove freshness, cannot inherit a specific
        locality, cannot claim a specific shape. Discharges the
        soundness floor for non-returning functions.

      (P6) Single-path identity:
        N = 1 -> r = rc_1 (componentwise)
        per the per-dim lattice-join identity for finite chains:
        `max(x) = x` over a singleton list, AND of a singleton
        bool list = that bool. Discharges the soundness floor for
        functions with exactly one Return terminator (most
        functions in practice).

  Proof obligation:
    Six-part discharge corresponding to (P1)..(P6); per-dim
    conjuncts (P1)..(P4) ground in the shipped Rust line-by-line
    structural match to the spec; lattice-property conjuncts
    (P5)..(P6) ground in lemmas L-1, L-2, L-4, L-6 from §AIMS §3
    plus the empty-fold identity for the CONSERVATIVE seed.

    Per-dimension parts (P1)..(P4) — uniform structural match:

      The shipped `extract_return_info` at extract.rs lines
      790-839 reads (collapsed to the load-bearing fold loop;
      the `locality` + `shape` dims default to CONSERVATIVE per
      target-vs-shipped axis carve-out):

        let mut return_uniqueness = None::<Uniqueness>;
        let mut all_preserve_freshness = true;

        for block in &func.blocks {
            if let ArcTerminator::Return { value } = &block.terminator {
                let (uniq, preserves) = var_uniqueness(
                    *value, &def_map, &invoke_defs,
                    &param_vars, classifier, sigs,
                );

                return_uniqueness = Some(match return_uniqueness {
                    None => uniq,
                    Some(prev) => prev.join(uniq),
                });

                if !preserves {
                    all_preserve_freshness = false;
                }
            }
        }

        match return_uniqueness {
            Some(uniqueness) => ReturnContract {
                uniqueness,
                preserves_freshness: all_preserve_freshness,
                returns_fresh_self_alloc:
                    saw_return && all_return_fresh_self_alloc,
                ..ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE
            },
            // No Return terminators — conservative.
            None => ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE,
        }

      Each per-dim operation is verified independently below.

      Part (P1) — Uniqueness max-join over Return paths:

        Line 821 reads:
          `Some(prev) => prev.join(uniq)`
        where `Uniqueness::join` is `std::cmp::max` over the
        canonical chain `Unique < MaybeShared < Shared` per
        §AIMS §3.4 (proven in sec-02 lattice properties).

        Per L-1 (commutativity) + L-2 (associativity), the
        fold-order-independent N-ary join is well-defined for
        any ordering of `Return` terminators (sec-6 IA-9
        "permutation-invariant"). Block visitation order in
        `func.blocks` does NOT affect the converged
        `return_uniqueness` value. (P1) discharged.

        Coherence side-condition: per-path uniqueness classified
        per Annex E §AIMS.9 Return Provenance table.
        Fresh-allocation paths (`Construct`, `Reuse`,
        `CollectionReuse`, `PartialApply`) yield `Unique` (the
        join identity); `Apply`/`Invoke` with contract inherit
        callee uniqueness; `Apply` no-contract /
        `ApplyIndirect` / `Project` / `Select` / unresolvable
        yield `MaybeShared`. Spec-side `Shared` never produced
        directly — it can only arise via callee contract
        inheritance, which IS sound (max-join with `Shared`
        from a callee correctly widens the function's return
        contract).

      Part (P2) — preserves_freshness AND over all paths:

        Line 824-826 reads:
          if !preserves {
              all_preserve_freshness = false;
          }

        This is the AND-monoid fold: `all_preserve_freshness`
        starts at the AND-identity `true` (line 806), and any
        path with `preserves == false` clears it permanently
        (sticky-false transition). Discharges the spec rule
        "Freshness requires ALL paths preserve" (sec-5 IC-4):

          all_preserve_freshness = AND over all paths of
                                   path.preserves_freshness

        Per L-1 (commutativity of AND on bool) + L-2
        (associativity of AND on bool), fold-order independent.
        (P2) discharged.

        `preserves_freshness` is not a self-allocation proof. Parameter
        passthrough and consumed-storage strategies may preserve uniqueness
        conditionally, so RL-29 does not consume this field.

      Part (P2a) — returns_fresh_self_alloc AND over all paths:

        The extractor begins optimistically and clears
        all_return_fresh_self_alloc whenever a returned value is not in the
        function-rooted fresh lineage. Parameter roots, projections, sharing
        views, and consumed-storage primitive results clear it. A direct fresh
        construct or transitively certified callee return may preserve it.

        This second AND fold is the provenance guarantee RL-29 consumes. The
        distinction is load-bearing: `(preserves_freshness=true,
        uniqueness=Unique)` may still describe storage the caller supplied.

      Part (P3) — Locality componentwise max-join:

        The shipped extractor defaults `locality` to
        `CONSERVATIVE.locality` (target-vs-shipped axis
        carve-out) — the `..ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE` spread
        at line 834 sets it on every emission.

        The spec join shape is: per-path locality classified per
        Annex E §AIMS.9 table (`BlockLocal` for
        Construct/Reuse/CollectionReuse/PartialApply,
        `HeapEscaping` for Project, callee's locality for
        Apply-with-contract, `Unknown` otherwise), joined via
        `std::cmp::max` over the 5-element chain per §AIMS §3.5.

        When the locality extractor lands, the same join
        shape applies — per L-1 + L-2 + L-6, the per-dim
        max-join is commutative, associative, and monotone over
        the locality chain. Empty-fold identity (N = 0) yields
        `CONSERVATIVE.locality = Unknown` per (P5). Single-path
        identity (N = 1) yields `rc_1.locality` per (P6).

        Target-vs-shipped axis carve-out: until the locality
        extractor lands, every emission carries
        `CONSERVATIVE.locality = Unknown`, which is the TOP of
        the locality chain and the most-conservative bound
        downstream consumers can assume (per L-6 monotonicity,
        cannot under-approximate; only ever over-approximates).
        (P3) discharged in proof shape; concrete fold deferred
        to the locality-extractor commit.

      Part (P4) — Shape componentwise join:

        Same argument as (P3): shipped extractor defaults
        `shape` to `CONSERVATIVE.shape` (`NonReusable`); spec
        join shape per §AIMS §7 + §AIMS §3.6 (ShapeClass-join over
        the per-path shape values from the classification
        table).

        ShapeClass join semantics per §AIMS §3.6 ShapeClass
        lattice: `ReusableCtor` and `CollectionBuffer` are
        Shape-equivalent under join only when both paths agree;
        any mixed pair (e.g., `ReusableCtor(Struct) join
        ReusableCtor(EnumVariant)`) joins to `NonReusable` per
        CN-3 canonicalization. Empty-fold identity yields
        `CONSERVATIVE.shape = NonReusable`. Single-path identity
        yields `rc_1.shape`.

        Target-vs-shipped axis carve-out: until the shape
        extractor lands, every emission carries
        `CONSERVATIVE.shape = NonReusable`, which is the TOP of
        the shape lattice for reusability and the
        most-conservative bound. (P4) discharged in proof
        shape.

      Coherence side-conditions for (P1)..(P4):

        - CN-3 (sec-2): `Uniqueness = Shared ∧ Shape ≠
          NonReusable ⟹ Shape := NonReusable`. Application
          after IC-4 join: if (P1) max-join yields `Shared`
          AND (P4) join yields a reusable shape, CN-3 clamps
          shape to `NonReusable` in a post-join canonicalization
          round. Per L-8 "Canonicalization SHALL preserve joins:
          `canonicalize(a ⊔ b) = a ⊔ b`" — the clamp does NOT
          change the join's semantic identity; it only maps the
          result into the canonical-state subset (cross-dim
          feasibility per sec-2 opener). IC-4 remains the join
          operator regardless of CN-3's post-processing.

        - CN-6 (sec-2 + §AIMS §7 exception): `Locality ≥
          HeapEscaping ∧ Uniqueness = Unique ⟹ Uniqueness :=
          MaybeShared` does NOT fire on ReturnContract per
          §AIMS §7 "ReturnContract locality" paragraph + "CN-6
          notes" — the extractor traces STRUCTURALLY, NOT per-
          variable lattice state, so CN-6's per-variable demote
          does not apply to the contract export. Return uniqueness remains a
          semantic contract dimension, while RL-29 independently consumes
          returns_fresh_self_alloc. This carve-out is
          load-bearing for the AIMS mission ("RC rare not RC
          fast" per Annex E §AIMS §1) — without it, returned
          fresh allocations would lose useful return-semantics precision at
          the function boundary.

        - CN-1 (sec-2): `Consumption = Dead ⟺ Cardinality =
          Absent` does NOT apply to ReturnContract — those are
          ParamContract dimensions, not ReturnContract
          dimensions. ReturnContract carries return semantics and provenance,
          including the distinct returns_fresh_self_alloc fact.

    Part (P5) — Empty-fold identity discharge:

      Goal: `N = 0 ⟹ r = ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE`.

      Lines 805 + 819-822 + 830-837 implement the empty-fold
      identity explicitly:

        let mut return_uniqueness = None::<Uniqueness>; // line 805
        // ... fold loop runs 0 times since no Return terminator ...
        match return_uniqueness {
            Some(uniqueness) => ReturnContract { uniqueness, ... },
            None => ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE, // line 837
        }

      `return_uniqueness == None` after the loop iff N = 0,
      precisely when the function has no `Return { value }`
      terminator. Examples: `loop { ... }` non-terminating
      function, function ending in `panic!`/`unreachable`/
      `todo` (which lower to `ArcTerminator::Unreachable` or
      similar, not `Return`). Returns
      `ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE = (uniqueness: MaybeShared,
      preserves_freshness: false, returns_fresh_self_alloc: false,
      locality: Unknown, shape: NonReusable)`.

      This is the soundness floor: downstream consumers seeing
      a CONSERVATIVE return contract cannot assume freshness,
      cannot assume a fresh-self-allocation proof, cannot inherit a specific locality
      or shape. The CONSERVATIVE seed exists to ENSURE that
      empty-return-set functions never accidentally widen a
      caller's optimistic claims.

      Per Birkhoff (Lattice Theory, 3rd ed., 1967, §I.5) the
      identity element of a finite-chain join is the TOP of the
      chain when the join is the empty fold over a list with no
      "natural" bottom seed — IC-4 uses TOP precisely so the
      empty fold is the strongest-conservative bound. (P5)
      discharged.

    Part (P6) — Single-path identity discharge:

      Goal: `N = 1 ⟹ r = rc_1` (componentwise).

      Lines 819-822 implement the single-path identity:
        return_uniqueness = Some(match return_uniqueness {
            None => uniq, // line 820 — N = 1 case
            Some(prev) => prev.join(uniq),
        });

      First iteration of the fold: `return_uniqueness == None`
      pre-iteration, so the match arm at line 820 fires,
      yielding `Some(uniq)`. No subsequent iterations (N = 1),
      so `return_uniqueness = Some(rc_1.uniqueness)` at fold
      exit.

      Similarly: `all_preserve_freshness` starts at `true` (line
      806). Line 824-826 clears it only if `!preserves`. For
      N = 1, the single path either preserves (no clear) or
      doesn't (clear). Final value = `rc_1.preserves_freshness`
      (by AND-monoid identity: `true ∧ x = x`).

      The same identity applies independently to
      returns_fresh_self_alloc: one certified self-allocation path yields true;
      one caller-rooted or consumed-storage path yields false.

      Per L-4 (partial-order axiom): `a ≤ b ⟺ a ⊔ b = b`. The
      single-path case is the trivial case of the partial-order
      reflexivity: `rc_1 ⊔ ∅ = rc_1` (where ∅ is the AND-monoid
      identity for `preserves_freshness` and the None-seed for
      `uniqueness`). (P6) discharged.

    Convergence within the SCC fixpoint:

      IC-4 does NOT iterate by itself — it is a per-function
      one-shot extraction that runs once per function per
      iteration of the SCC fixpoint loop (PL-1a). The N-ary
      join over `Return` terminators is a finite fold; per-dim
      operators each have finite height (Uniqueness 2, Locality
      4, Shape 1, and two path-universal booleans; the formal convergence
      bound includes the full ReturnContract carrier per
      Annex E §AIMS.8 L-5).

      IC-7 (proven separately) bounds the outer SCC fixpoint
      iteration count at `param_count × 13 + 8 + 6 + 4`,
      where the `8` term is the per-function ReturnContract
      height budget (spec formula per Annex E §AIMS
      IC-7). Each iteration of the SCC loop re-runs IC-4 once
      per function and re-runs IC-3 join for every observed
      call-site demand-flow event; ReturnContract advances at
      most 8 times per function before TOP is reached.
      Convergence is therefore guaranteed by IC-7's bound.

    Coverage gate — corpus enumeration:

      The shipped `extract_return_info` is exercised by the
      interprocedural test surface in
      compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/interprocedural/
      tests.rs + adjacent test modules. Each row below pins
      one or more (P1)..(P6) conjuncts:

        Row R1: no_return_terminators —
                function with `loop { }` body or
                panic-terminated body; IC-4 returns
                CONSERVATIVE; (P5) pin
        Row R2: single_construct_return —
                `Return Construct(...)`; IC-4 returns
                `(Unique, true, true, BlockLocal, ReusableCtor)`
                semantically; (P6) + (P1) + (P2a) positive pin
        Row R3: single_param_passthrough —
                `Return param`; may preserve freshness but sets
                returns_fresh_self_alloc=false; (P6) + (P2a) negative pin
        Row R4: select_construct_or_construct —
                `Return Select(_, Construct(A), Construct(B))`;
                both branches fresh; (P1) Unique-join +
                (P2) AND-of-trues = true + (P4) Shape-join
                across two ReusableCtor pin
        Row R5: select_construct_or_apply_no_contract —
                `Return Select(_, Construct, Apply-no-ctx)`;
                fresh path AND non-fresh path; (P2) AND-of-mixed
                = false sticky-clear pin
        Row R6: two_returns_both_fresh —
                two basic blocks both ending in `Return
                Construct(...)`; (P1) max-join of identical
                Unique + (P2) AND-of-trues = true pin
        Row R7: two_returns_one_fresh_one_param —
                one block returns Construct, another returns
                param; (P1) max-join Unique ⊔ MaybeShared =
                MaybeShared + (P2) AND-of-mixed = false pin
        Row R8: apply_with_unique_callee —
                `Return Apply(callee)` where callee has
                `ReturnContract.uniqueness = Unique` and
                `preserves_freshness = true`; inherited per
                §AIMS §7 table; (P1) + (P2) inheritance pin
        Row R9: apply_with_maybeshared_callee —
                callee has `MaybeShared / false`; downgrade
                inherited; (P1) widen pin
        Row R10: invoke_unique_return_through_terminator —
                Invoke terminator with Unique-return contract;
                exercises `invoke_defs` path lines 903-905;
                terminator-defined-var pin
        Row R11: let_var_chain_to_construct —
                `Return let_var` where let-var chains via Let
                { Var(v) } to a Construct; transparent-alias
                trace per §AIMS §7 "Tracing rules" bullet 1;
                Let-chain depth-tracing pin
        Row R12: scc_fixpoint_iterate_return —
                mutual-recursion SCC where A's
                ReturnContract depends on B's and vice versa;
                multi-iteration convergence; full IC-3 + IC-4 +
                IC-7 interaction pin
        Row R13: empty_fold_versus_single_conservative —
                contrast Row R1 (no return; CONSERVATIVE seed)
                vs. function explicitly returning a value with
                conservative-from-classification result;
                distinguishes empty-fold from single-path-with-
                conservative-classification

      Coverage axes:
        Per-dim correctness (P1)..(P4):
          Rows R2 through R12 directly pin each dimension
          across freshness-preserving + freshness-clearing +
          callee-inherited + parameter-passthrough cases
        Lattice properties (P5)..(P6):
          Rows R1 + R13 pin empty-fold; Rows R2 + R3 pin
          single-path identity; multi-path cases (R4-R7)
          exercise the fold loop
        Multi-iteration soundness:
          Row R12 pins IC-4's role inside the SCC fixpoint
          loop (combined with IC-3 and IC-7)
        Coherence interaction:
          CN-3 + CN-6 §AIMS §7 carve-out exercised implicitly by
          fresh-allocation rows (R2, R4, R6, R8)

    Negative-direction witness — replacing AND with OR on
    returns_fresh_self_alloc rejected:

      Take one self-allocation return path and one parameter-passthrough or
      consumed-storage path. An OR fold would report
      returns_fresh_self_alloc=true even though one result may alias storage
      already visible to the caller. RL-29 would then freeze Proven and an LLVM
      direct-pointer projection could emit return noalias unsoundly.

      The path-universal AND is therefore load-bearing. One non-self-allocation
      path must sticky-clear the neutral fact before any backend projection.

    Negative-direction witness — empty-fold yielding
    OPTIMISTIC rejected:

      Take Row R1 (no_return_terminators). Suppose hypothetical
      implementer replaces the None-arm at line 837 with
      `ReturnContract::OPTIMISTIC` instead of
      `ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE`:
        None => ReturnContract {
            uniqueness: Uniqueness::Unique,
            preserves_freshness: true,
            returns_fresh_self_alloc: true,
            ..ReturnContract::CONSERVATIVE
        },

      Then a non-returning function (`loop { }`, panic-only,
      `unreachable!`-only) would emit a ReturnContract claiming
      `Unique` + both boolean facts true. Callers
      consuming this function's contract via TF-6 (refine)
      would receive a Unique-Unique-fresh return value
      classification — but the function never actually returns
      a value, so the lattice "refined" claim is meaningless.
      RL-29 would also receive an unsupported Proven fact despite there being
      no returned allocation witness.

      Per Tarski (Pacific J. Math., 1955) least-fixpoint
      theorem: the iteration sequence converges to the LUB of
      its initial state and observed demands. An OPTIMISTIC
      empty-fold violates the precondition that empty cases
      yield the conservative bound; downstream consumers then
      believe a fact that is not supported by any actual return
      path. CONSERVATIVE-on-empty is the soundness floor;
      OPTIMISTIC-on-empty is a soundness failure.

    Negative-direction witness — non-monotone uniqueness join
    rejected:

      Take Row R7 (two_returns_one_fresh_one_param). Suppose
      hypothetical implementer makes the Uniqueness join
      anti-monotone (e.g., `min` instead of `max`):
        return_uniqueness = Some(match return_uniqueness {
            None => uniq,
            Some(prev) => prev.min(uniq), // min instead of max
        });

      Joining one path's `Unique` with another path's
      `MaybeShared` would yield `Unique` (min of
      Unique < MaybeShared). Downstream: caller TF-6 refines
      its return-value variable to `Unique`, even though one
      of the function's actual return paths produces a value
      that may be shared with the caller's parameter (the
      passthrough path).

      Per L-6 (monotonicity): `a ≤ b ⟹ f(a) ≤ f(b)`. The
      corrupted min-join is anti-monotone — joining with a
      strictly larger Uniqueness makes the result SMALLER. The
      converged contract under-approximates observed return-path demands; some
      return paths are silently dropped from the lattice's view. The distinct
      RL-29 provenance fact prevents this uniqueness error from being mistaken
      for a noalias proof, but the contract remains unsound.

      L-6 monotonicity is therefore load-bearing for the
      entire ReturnContract discipline; min-join (or any
      anti-monotone join) is a soundness foundation violation.

    IC-4 dependencies — leaf within AIMS return-contract layer:

      IC-4 consumes per-instr classification data (§AIMS §7
      Return Provenance table), the converged callee
      ContractMap (filled in by prior SCC iterations + IC-3),
      and per-dim `join` operators (proven in sec-02 lattice
      properties). It produces a per-function ReturnContract
      assignment. The dependency chain at the proof-obligation
      level is acyclic:
        IC-1 --> IC-2 (parameter contract seeding)
        IC-2 --> IC-3 (parameter contract componentwise join)
        IC-3 --> IC-4 (return contract componentwise join,
                         uses same join shape applied to
                         ReturnContract's semantic/provenance tuple)
        L-1/L-2/L-4/L-6/L-8 (sec-02 lattice properties) --> IC-4
